  [38;5;153mbilling         4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   failing: db-connection[39m
  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   failing: disk-space, index-freshness[39m
  [38;5;153mauth            1.8.0     2/2     2026-09-30 07:15   ok[39m
  Easy to scan, and it's still one row per service. The details are left out, and the column can get wide when several
  checks fail.

  [1mB. Short HEALTH column, details listed under the table[0m
  [38;5;153mSERVICE         VERSION   READY   DEPLOYED           HEALTH[39m
  [38;5;153mbilling         4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   1 failing[39m
  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   2 failing[39m
  [38;5;153mauth            1.8.0     2/2     2026-09-30 07:15   ok[39m

  [38;5;153mFailing checks:[39m
    [38;5;153mbilling  db-connection     timeout after 5s[39m
    [38;5;153msearch   disk-space        91% used[39m
    [38;5;153msearch   index-freshness   last update 47 min ago[39m
  The table stays narrow and you get the full details. The output is longer, but only when something is failing.

  [1mC. Details on indented lines under each failing row[0m
  Everything sits next to its service, but rows stop being one line each, which makes the table harder to scan and to
  [38;5;153mgrep[39m.

  [1mMy recommendation is B.[0m The column answers "which services are failing?" at a glance, and the detail is what you need
  next. Whichever you choose, the snapshot order stays the same and the existing columns don't change.
